A||N||11||MickeyAsh||Timing, fuel pump?||||9||
Z||000000||MickeyAsh||11-06-2000||03:31 AM||||I'll just throw it out there and see if I get any bites. =D So, when I first go to crank my GT in the morning, it never fires up on the first try, no matter how long I hold the key over. Yes, I let the fuel pump run til it stops, every time, to no avail. Interestingly enough, however, if I turn it over and let the pump run for a while, then turn the key to "off", then wait a while and turn it back to "on" and let the pump run again, and THEN crank it, it fires up with little effort. Also, I have no problem when it comes to starting the engine in the middle of the day... only in the mornings after not driving it for a while. Another thing it does is sometimes after leaving it for a couple of hours, after cranking, it hesitates and lopes when I floor it. It randomly hesitates when I floor it at lights and stop signs as well... any ideas? Thanks in advance.||||reg||9
Z||000001||88formula||11-06-2000||05:15 PM||||That's easy, I think your cold start injector is junk. A coolant temperature sensor is about $10 and has allot to do with cold starts also so I would just replace this too.  Make sure your car is tuned up and another thing that helps allot is cleaning the throttle opening. To do this just buy a can of carburetor cleaner and spray about ¼ of the can into the throttle body while the engine is running at about 2000 rpm. Than shut the engine down and spray the backside of the throttle blade and intake opening real well. Let the car sit for about 20-minutes and than start it up. You will have trouble starting it but when it does you will be amazed at how much crap comes out of the tailpipe.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||11-06-2000||05:20 PM||||I just remembered that the cold start injector relies on the coolant temperature sensor for its operation. I would just replace the coolant temperature sensor first and see what that does before tackling the cold start injector.||||reg||1
Z||000003||MickeyAsh||11-06-2000||05:43 PM||||Sounds right for the morning stuff... but how about the hesitation at lights? It's gonna end up getting me killed because sometimes it does it and sometimes it doesnt, and if it hesitates in front of a semi, I'm screwed.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||11-06-2000||05:55 PM||||88, ...think you meant Cold Start switch, not the coolant temperature sensor.<P>Anyway, I suppose that doesn't explain the stalling problem, either.  Might try changing the fuel filter, unless you just did it last month.  Pure guess, of course.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||MickeyAsh||11-06-2000||06:17 PM||||I just changed the filter literally last month, in the middle of a short spree of mishaps, I changed a ton of stuff. (Fuel filter, Air filter, pick-up coil, distributor, dist. cap, spark plugs, plug wires, ignition coil, ignition module, o2 sensor, MAP sensor) I dont think I'm leaving anything out... lol... needless to say I'm getting nice gas mileage, but I'm still worried about the hesitation... a thought that crossed my mind is that my idle is never stable either... right after cranking, it idles from 1200 - 1500, and in gear it's 900 - 1200. On occasion in drive, idles right on the money between 900 - 1000, but it's never stable, always jumping.||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||11-06-2000||07:44 PM||||StuGood your right I think. I don't really remember because it has been a long time since I looked at one of those. MickeyAsh does your fiero pop and backfire when you floor it and then go? <P>Another sensor you may want to check is the TPS (throttle position sensor) sensor. This sensor is like the accelerator pump on a carburetor. It tells the computer to richen the fuel mixture the moment you apply the throttle until the map sensor picks up the drop in vacuum upon throttle opening and tells the computer the engines under load. The map sensor is like the power valve. To test your TPS use an analog ohmmeter. Back probe the ground wire and sensor wire, not the voltage wire. Swipe the TPS slowly and there should be a smooth increase in resistance. If it spikes it’s no good. Also check the voltage drop at WOT with the ignition on and the car not running. It should be close the reference voltage of five volts using a voltmeter.<P>The TPS sensor can cause a hesitation if it has a break in it or it’s worn. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||MickeyAsh||11-06-2000||09:34 PM||||It used to backfire, but that problem went away after replacing the ign. module... however, if I rev the engine, it sputters and pops while it revs down... sounds kinda like a dirtbike does when you lean into it... I can't think of anything that would cause a hesitation when I floor it besides the TPS...||||reg||1
Z||000008||jazer||11-07-2000||12:24 AM||||My girlfriends 90 sunbird does the exact same thing to the "T".  Her car doesn't have a cold start injector.  I would check the fuel pressure.  Leave the gauges on all day because you might have a very slow pressure drop caused by your fuel pump.  Also, your oil pressure sensor can be going because it controls your fuel pressure as well.  The reason it starts when you do the on-off-on ritual is because you pressurize the system.  Also, the reason it usually occurs in the morning is because the pressure drop is so slow that it takes the over-night sitting for the pressure to drop.  A suggestion, if you need to replace your fuel pump, go with original parts.  Some aftermarket pumps aren't as strong.||||reg||1
Z||000009||jazer||11-07-2000||12:31 AM||||I definately think your fuel pump is going because it sounds like you're not getting enough fuel when accelerating causing the lack of power and hesitaion.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||11-07-2000||01:27 AM||||The fuel pressure regulator is also suppose to hold fuel in the fuel rails when the car sits. If the fuel bleeds down it dries out the regulator and could be why it is hard to start. This could also cause hesitation if the regulator is messed up. I would try the regulator first before the fuel pump. (cheaper and easier).<BR> Lets us know what you find.||||reg||1
Z||000011||MickeyAsh||11-07-2000||12:22 PM||||I'm starting the project thursday, gotta get paid so I can buy a new haynes manual and parts... I'll keep you guys updated if I make some headway... thanks for your help.||||reg||1
